      "title": "Read me",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## The fast path: distilled LoRA, 8 steps, cfg 1.0\n\n**Measured on an RTX 3090, 960x544x121, seed 42, warm:**\n\n| Config | Steps | s/step | Wall |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline, cfg 3.0, SDPA | 20 | 11.04 | 230.8 s |\n| This graph (sage) | 8 | 10.68 | **94.3 s** |\n\n**2.45x end to end, and the output is sharper** than the 20-step baseline. Run\nthe same 8 steps with the LoRA bypassed and you get unusable mush, so it is the\nLoRA doing the work, not the step count.\n\nThe sigma list is upstream's, from\n`LTX-2.3_T2V_I2V_Single_Stage_Distilled_Full.json`: 9 sigmas = 8 steps.\n\ncfg 1.0 is also the path this fork's Phase 0 fix unblocked -- `noise_pred_neg`\nand `noise_pred_perturbed` used to be bound only inside conditional branches, so\na distilled run raised `UnboundLocalError` before it could produce a frame.\n\n## SageAttention is the small part\n\n`LTXV Attention Backend` -> `sage` is worth about **4%** here (11.07 -> 10.68\ns/step). The isolated kernel is ~2.2x faster than SDPA, but that does not carry:\na cfg-3.0 step costs the same as a cfg-1.0 step on this model, which means the\nper-forward cost is dominated by dequantising 11.8 GB of GGUF weights rather\nthan by arithmetic. **Weight bandwidth is the bottleneck, so fewer steps is the\nlever, not faster steps.**\n\nSet `backend = default` to turn it off. It is an INT8 approximation (latents\ndiffer from SDPA by relative L2 ~0.038 over a full render, visually equivalent).\n\n**Caveat:** masked attention falls back to SDPA and gets nothing at all. The\ninstalled sageattention 2.2.0 takes no `attn_mask`, and LTX's guide-mask path\nadditionally passes `low_precision_attention=False`. So any guide with\n`strength != 1.0` or a pixel mask sees none of it.\n\n**LoRA strength:** 1.0 works well here. Upstream's two-stage example uses 0.5 and\n0.2 for a differently-packaged variant of the same distilled family, so if your\nLoRA file differs from the one named in the loader, try those.\n\n## Why this graph looks like it does\n\nTwo things in the earlier version of these workflows could never have run, and\nboth were found by actually executing them:\n\n**1. The text encoder.** `ltx-2-3-22b-text_encoder.safetensors` is *not* a text\nencoder -- it holds four tensors, `text_embedding_projection.*`. LTX-2.3's text\nencoder is **Gemma-3-12B**, and the projection aggregates its 49 hidden states\n(49 x 3840 = 188160). `comfy/sd.py` only takes the LTX-2.3 Gemma branch\n(`ltxav_te` + `LTXAVGemmaTokenizer`, sd.py:1809) when handed **exactly two**\nstate dicts. A single-file `CLIPLoader(type=ltxv)` falls into the LTX-0.9 **T5**\nbranch instead and dies with `Cannot copy out of meta tensor`.\n\nSo: **`DualCLIPLoader`, type `ltxv`**, Gemma first (sd.py:1812 reads the\ntokenizer from `clip_data[0][\"spiece_model\"]`), projection second. Both live in\n`models/text_encoders`, so nothing is needed in `models/checkpoints`.\n\n**2. The audio branch is not optional.** `ltx-2.3-22b-dev` is an **AV** DiT.\n`av_model.py` builds audio RoPE unconditionally, so a video-only latent gives it\nzero audio tokens and it crashes in `freqs_cis_matrix`. Every upstream 2.3\nworkflow concatenates an audio latent for exactly this reason.\n`LTXVAudioVAELoader` lists from the (empty) `checkpoints` folder, but core\n`VAELoader` reads `models/vae` -- where `LTX23_audio_vae_bf16.safetensors` is.\nThe audio half is split back off and discarded here.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
